Subject: [PATCH] dwc2: bring host mode up after a single IDDIG debounce on role-switch platforms
Upstream-Status: Pending

An accessory-initiated role switch on the Carlinkit Mini Ultra (Ingenic
X1600, DWC2 core 3.00a, IDDIG filter present, no bypass on this core
revision) costs two debounce periods before the root hub port is
powered:

  force_mode(host)                                   t=0
  dwc2_conn_id_status_change()                       +100ms  core is host
  dwc2_core_init(false) -> dwc2_core_reset()                 core is device again
  dwc2_wait_for_mode(host)                           +200ms
  Init: Port Power applied

The first period is the hardware filter reacting to the force-mode
write. The second is self-inflicted: dwc2_core_init(false) soft-resets
the core, and as dwc2_core_reset() documents, a soft reset puts the core
back into device mode until the filter has run once more. On top of
that, dwc2_force_mode() itself polls for the mode change first, so the
caller is blocked for the first period as well.

A Continental NAC Wave 2 (Stellantis/Toyota) does not wait that long.
After it sends the role-switch request and sees us disconnect, it
becomes a device, pulls up D+ and waits for a bus reset - for roughly
200ms. The vendor firmware has its port powered ~100ms after forcing
host mode and the head unit enumerated 300ms later. With the stock flow
the port is powered at ~200ms and no device is ever seen on it (24
HPRT0 samples over six seconds, CONNSTS=0 every time, every capture),
and when the dongle returns to device mode the head unit re-enumerates
it at once: it had long gone back to host mode.

Fold the two debounce periods into one, on role-switch platforms only
(dr_mode OTG with a usb-role-switch property, the condition the existing
dwc2_clear_force_mode() special case already keys on):

 * dwc2_force_mode() no longer polls for the mode change on these
   platforms. dwc2_conn_id_status_change() is what brings the mode up,
   and nothing consumed the poll result.

 * dwc2_drd_role_sw_set() soft-resets the core itself, right after the
   force-mode write, with skip_wait. The debounce period the reset
   restarts is then the one the force-mode write costs anyway.
   Force-mode bits and GOTGCTL session overrides survive the reset.
   Since the reset clears GAHBCFG.GLBL_INTR_EN and GINTMSK, no Connector
   ID Status Change interrupt will follow; the handler is queued
   directly instead.

 * DCTL.SftDiscon is set before the force-mode write and again right
   after the reset. DCTL's reset value is "connected" (which is why
   dwc2_gadget_init() sets SftDiscon after the probe-time reset); left
   alone, the core re-presents a pull-up for the ~100ms it spends in
   device mode after the reset, and the head unit - still a host at that
   point - sees disconnect/reconnect/disconnect where it expects one
   disconnect before it swaps roles. This was found the hard way: a
   version of this patch without it moved the reset and got no head
   unit; a version without any reset got the head unit but could not
   enumerate it (host without a core reset after a mode change does
   not transfer).

 * dwc2_conn_id_status_change() decides A/B on the GUSBCFG force bits
   rather than the filtered connector ID (which still reads B when the
   work runs), polls for host mode at 1-2ms instead of 20ms, and calls
   dwc2_core_init(hsotg, true), i.e. without a second reset.
   initial_setup=true also passes select_phy=true to dwc2_phy_init();
   dwc2_hs_phy_init() only writes GUSBCFG (and only then resets) if the
   PHY selection differs from what is programmed, and PHYIF16 survives
   the reset, so it does not.

Bench (sysfs role switch, no head unit): port powered 100.4ms after the
force instead of 201ms; the B path back to device mode still resets and
reconnects as before; interrupts keep counting across the cycle.

Car (NAC Wave 2): the head unit has pulled up D+ by the time the port
is powered, every time, and with the two follow-up patches enumerates
as "Continental OIP" and completes the iAP2 accessory probe.

---
 drivers/usb/dwc2/core.c | 11 +++++++
 drivers/usb/dwc2/drd.c  | 60 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 drivers/usb/dwc2/hcd.c  | 64 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-------
 3 files changed, 126 insertions(+), 9 deletions(-)

--- a/drivers/usb/dwc2/core.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/dwc2/core.c
@@ -576,6 +576,18 @@
 		 dwc2_readl(hsotg, GINTSTS), dwc2_readl(hsotg, GINTMSK),
 		 dwc2_readl(hsotg, GRSTCTL));
 
+	/*
+	 * On role-switch platforms (dr_mode OTG, no external ID pin) the
+	 * write above raises a Connector ID Status Change interrupt once the
+	 * IDDIG debounce filter has run, and dwc2_conn_id_status_change() is
+	 * what brings the new mode up. Polling here as well only blocks the
+	 * caller for that same debounce period - and for the host direction
+	 * dwc2_drd_role_sw_set() has work to do in that window, see there.
+	 */
+	if (hsotg->dr_mode == USB_DR_MODE_OTG &&
+	    device_property_read_bool(hsotg->dev, "usb-role-switch"))
+		return;
+
 	dwc2_wait_for_mode(hsotg, host);
 	return;
 }
--- a/drivers/usb/dwc2/drd.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/dwc2/drd.c
@@ -83,6 +83,66 @@
 	return 0;
 }
 
+/*
+ * Device -> host on a role-switch platform, stock flow:
+ *
+ *   force-mode write ... IDDIG debounce (100ms) ... ConIDStsChng IRQ
+ *   -> dwc2_conn_id_status_change() -> dwc2_core_init(false)
+ *   -> core soft reset -> core drops back to device mode
+ *   ... IDDIG debounce (100ms) again ... -> dwc2_hcd_start()
+ *
+ * Two debounce periods before the root hub port is powered, and only the
+ * second one is a real requirement: mainline resets the core on every mode
+ * change so host init starts from a known state, and the filter restarts on
+ * every reset. Fold them: reset the core right here, immediately after the
+ * force-mode write, so the debounce period the reset restarts is the one
+ * the force-mode write costs anyway. The force-mode bits and the GOTGCTL
+ * session overrides survive a soft reset (see dwc2_core_reset() and
+ * dwc2_ovr_avalid()), so nothing about the requested mode is lost.
+ *
+ * The reset also clears GAHBCFG.GLBL_INTR_EN and GINTMSK, so no Connector
+ * ID Status Change interrupt is going to announce the mode change. Queue
+ * the handler ourselves: it polls for host mode, skips its own reset on
+ * this platform and brings the HCD up - ~100ms after the force-mode write
+ * instead of ~200ms.
+ *
+ * For one debounce period after the reset the core sits in device mode
+ * with DCTL at its reset value, and that value is "connected" (which is
+ * why dwc2_gadget_init() sets SftDiscon right after the probe-time reset).
+ * Left alone, the core re-presents a pull-up for ~100ms right after we
+ * dropped it, and a head unit that is still a host at this point sees
+ * disconnect / reconnect / disconnect where it expects a single disconnect
+ * before it swaps roles itself. So SftDiscon is set before the force-mode
+ * write and again right after the reset.
+ *
+ * Only ever called with role == USB_ROLE_HOST, dr_mode == OTG and the HCD
+ * initialised (wq_otg exists).
+ */
+static void dwc2_drd_early_host_reset(struct dwc2_hsotg *hsotg)
+{
+	unsigned long flags;
+	int ret;
+
+	spin_lock_irqsave(&hsotg->lock, flags);
+	dwc2_hsotg_disconnect(hsotg);
+	dwc2_hsotg_core_disconnect(hsotg);
+	spin_unlock_irqrestore(&hsotg->lock, flags);
+
+	ret = dwc2_core_reset(hsotg, true);
+	if (ret)
+		dev_err(hsotg->dev,
+			"role switch: early core reset failed: %d\n", ret);
+
+	dwc2_hsotg_core_disconnect(hsotg);
+
+	dev_info(hsotg->dev,
+		 "role switch: core reset folded into force-mode debounce, GOTGCTL=%08x GUSBCFG=%08x DCTL=%08x\n",
+		 dwc2_readl(hsotg, GOTGCTL), dwc2_readl(hsotg, GUSBCFG),
+		 dwc2_readl(hsotg, DCTL));
+
+	queue_work(hsotg->wq_otg, &hsotg->wf_otg);
+}
+
 static int dwc2_drd_role_sw_set(struct usb_role_switch *sw, enum usb_role role)
 {
 	struct dwc2_hsotg *hsotg = usb_role_switch_get_drvdata(sw);
@@ -181,10 +241,15 @@
 
 	spin_unlock_irqrestore(&hsotg->lock, flags);
 
-	if (!already && hsotg->dr_mode == USB_DR_MODE_OTG)
+	if (!already && hsotg->dr_mode == USB_DR_MODE_OTG) {
 		/* This will raise a Connector ID Status Change Interrupt */
 		dwc2_force_mode(hsotg, role == USB_ROLE_HOST);
 
+		/* ...except that for host we do not wait for it, see above. */
+		if (role == USB_ROLE_HOST && hsotg->wq_otg)
+			dwc2_drd_early_host_reset(hsotg);
+	}
+
 	if (!hsotg->ll_hw_enabled && hsotg->clk)
 		clk_disable_unprepare(hsotg->clk);
 
--- a/drivers/usb/dwc2/hcd.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/dwc2/hcd.c
@@ -3141,6 +3141,7 @@
 	u32 count = 0;
 	u32 gotgctl;
 	unsigned long flags;
+	ktime_t start;
 
 	dev_dbg(hsotg->dev, "%s()\n", __func__);
 
@@ -3149,6 +3150,26 @@
 	dev_dbg(hsotg->dev, "gotgctl.b.conidsts=%d\n",
 		!!(gotgctl & GOTGCTL_CONID_B));
 
+	/*
+	 * On role-switch platforms the mode is dictated by the GUSBCFG force
+	 * bits, and the connector ID in GOTGCTL merely follows them - one
+	 * IDDIG debounce period later, and from device mode all over again
+	 * after every core soft reset. dwc2_drd_role_sw_set() queues this
+	 * work right after forcing host mode and resetting the core, i.e.
+	 * while the filtered ID still reads B. Decide on the force bits, so
+	 * the A path below gets to wait for host mode instead of the B path
+	 * happily re-initialising device mode.
+	 */
+	if (hsotg->dr_mode == USB_DR_MODE_OTG &&
+	    device_property_read_bool(hsotg->dev, "usb-role-switch")) {
+		u32 gusbcfg = dwc2_readl(hsotg, GUSBCFG);
+
+		if (gusbcfg & GUSBCFG_FORCEHOSTMODE)
+			gotgctl &= ~GOTGCTL_CONID_B;
+		else if (gusbcfg & GUSBCFG_FORCEDEVMODE)
+			gotgctl |= GOTGCTL_CONID_B;
+	}
+
 	/* B-Device connector (Device Mode) */
 	if (gotgctl & GOTGCTL_CONID_B) {
 		dwc2_vbus_supply_exit(hsotg);
@@ -3201,25 +3222,55 @@
 host:
 		/* A-Device connector (Host Mode) */
 		dev_dbg(hsotg->dev, "connId A\n");
+		/*
+		 * Poll at 1-2ms rather than 20ms: on role-switch platforms
+		 * this is the wait for the IDDIG debounce period restarted by
+		 * the early reset in dwc2_drd_role_sw_set(), and every
+		 * millisecond here is a millisecond the head unit sits as a
+		 * device without a host. Same ~5s budget as before.
+		 */
+		start = ktime_get();
 		while (!dwc2_is_host_mode(hsotg)) {
-			dev_info(hsotg->dev, "Waiting for Host Mode, Mode=%s\n",
-				 dwc2_is_host_mode(hsotg) ?
-				 "Host" : "Peripheral");
-			msleep(20);
-			if (++count > 250)
+			usleep_range(1000, 2000);
+			if (++count > 2500)
 				break;
 		}
-		if (count > 250)
+		if (count > 2500)
 			dev_err(hsotg->dev,
 				"Connection id status change timed out\n");
+		else if (count)
+			dev_info(hsotg->dev,
+				 "connId A: host mode after %lld ms\n",
+				 ktime_ms_delta(ktime_get(), start));
 
 		spin_lock_irqsave(&hsotg->lock, flags);
 		dwc2_hsotg_disconnect(hsotg);
 		spin_unlock_irqrestore(&hsotg->lock, flags);
 
 		hsotg->op_state = OTG_STATE_A_HOST;
-		/* Initialize the Core for Host mode */
-		dwc2_core_init(hsotg, false);
+
+		/*
+		 * Initialize the Core for Host mode.
+		 *
+		 * dwc2_core_init(hsotg, false) soft-resets the core, and per
+		 * dwc2_core_reset() a soft reset drops the core back into
+		 * device mode until the IDDIG debounce filter has run once
+		 * more. On role-switch platforms that reset has already been
+		 * done by dwc2_drd_role_sw_set(), right after the force-mode
+		 * write, so that its debounce period and the one the
+		 * force-mode write costs anyway are the same period.
+		 * Resetting again here would pay it twice.
+		 *
+		 * initial_setup=true is "do not reset, (re)select the PHY":
+		 * dwc2_hs_phy_init() only touches GUSBCFG (and only then
+		 * resets) if the PHY selection differs from what is already
+		 * programmed, which it never does at this point.
+		 */
+		if (hsotg->dr_mode == USB_DR_MODE_OTG &&
+		    device_property_read_bool(hsotg->dev, "usb-role-switch"))
+			dwc2_core_init(hsotg, true);
+		else
+			dwc2_core_init(hsotg, false);
 		dwc2_enable_global_interrupts(hsotg);
 		dwc2_hcd_start(hsotg);
 	}
